---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Onboarding & Certification System"
  description="Avery Moon designed a structured onboarding, OJT, assessment, and certification system for a live manufacturing environment."
>
<section class="case-hero case-training">
  <div class="container">
    <a class="case-back" href={`${base}/built-systems/`}>← Built Systems</a>
    <div class="case-kicker">06 / Learning Systems</div>
    <h1>Onboarding &<br/><em>Certification System</em></h1>
    <p class="case-deck">A structured development system that gave new and cross-training employees a clearer path from onboarding through supervised practice, assessment, and independent certification.</p>
    <div class="case-role-line"><span>Program Design</span><span>Training Architecture</span><span>Standards Design</span><span>Trainer Coordination</span><span>Implementation</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-metrics case-metrics-light">
  <div class="container case-metric-grid">
    <div><strong>8</strong><span>Employees in Initial Cohort</span></div>
    <div><strong>8 Weeks</strong><span>Core Onboarding Pathway</span></div>
    <div><strong>6–14 Weeks</strong><span>Certification Range</span></div>
    <div><strong>160 Hours</strong><span>Visual Training Required Before Final Test</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section">
  <div class="container case-two-col">
    <div>
      <div class="case-section-no">01</div>
      <div class="eyebrow">The Gap</div>
      <h2>Certification existed. The path to get there was inconsistent.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="case-copy">
      <p>After mandatory company onboarding, employees entered the department without a shared development plan. Training depended heavily on production demand, available trainers, and local knowledge, which made progress difficult for employees and supervisors to see.</p>
      <p>I built a repeatable structure around the existing Quality-owned certification process: defined training sequences, named trainers, weekly objectives, supervised practice, mock assessments, and target certification dates.</p>
      <p class="case-emphasis">The goal was not to make training rigid. It was to make expectations visible while leaving enough flexibility to work around a live production environment.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section training-evidence-path">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">02</div>
    <div class="eyebrow">How Development Progresses</div>
    <h2>A visible path from first day to demonstrated capability.</h2>
    <div class="training-simple-path reveal">
      <div><small>01</small><strong>Onboarding</strong><span>Role context, safety, systems, procedures</span></div>
      <b>→</b>
      <div><small>02</small><strong>Structured OJT</strong><span>Assigned trainers, practice, increasing responsibility</span></div>
      <b>→</b>
      <div><small>03</small><strong>Assessment</strong><span>Observation, feedback, mock testing, gap closure</span></div>
      <b>→</b>
      <div><small>04</small><strong>Certification</strong><span>Required training completed before final testing</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="case-note">The schedule could flex when attendance, trainer availability, or production needs changed without losing the employee's overall development path.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section case-alt training-document-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">03</div>
    <div class="eyebrow">From Plan to Practice</div>
    <h2>The documentation made development usable day to day.</h2>
    <p class="case-intro">The system connected a common onboarding foundation with individual training plans that identified who an employee would work with, what they would practice, and the milestone they were working toward.</p>

    <div class="training-doc-composition">
      <article class="training-paper training-paper-main">
        <div class="paper-kicker">Onboarding Plan</div>
        <h3>Structured foundation</h3>
        <div class="paper-rule"></div>
        <div class="paper-row"><span>Week 01</span><strong>Orientation + fundamentals</strong></div>
        <div class="paper-row"><span>Week 02</span><strong>Role-specific exposure</strong></div>
        <div class="paper-row"><span>Weeks 03–06</span><strong>Guided practice + broader work</strong></div>
        <div class="paper-row"><span>Weeks 07–08</span><strong>Readiness + targeted development</strong></div>
        <p>Common structure before and alongside role-specific training.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="training-paper training-paper-secondary">
        <div class="paper-kicker">Progressive Training Plan</div>
        <h3>Milestones with named support</h3>
        <div class="paper-rule"></div>
        <div class="paper-grid-head"><span>Period</span><span>Focus</span><span>Milestone</span></div>
        <div class="paper-grid-row"><span>Early</span><strong>Foundational work</strong><em>Supervised practice</em></div>
        <div class="paper-grid-row"><span>Middle</span><strong>Applied inspection / process work</strong><em>Increasing ownership</em></div>
        <div class="paper-grid-row"><span>Late</span><strong>Mock assessment + review</strong><em>Gap closure</em></div>
        <div class="paper-grid-row"><span>Target</span><strong>Certification testing</strong><em>Demonstrated readiness</em></div>
      </article>

      <div class="training-doc-callouts">
        <div><span>01</span><strong>Assigned trainers</strong><p>Employees knew who they were learning from rather than waiting for ad hoc direction.</p></div>
        <div><span>02</span><strong>Visible milestones</strong><p>Training plans connected practice time to mock tests, required hours, and certification targets.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section competency-transform">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">04</div>
    <div class="eyebrow">Defining Capability</div>
    <h2>From inherited standards to observable expectations.</h2>
    <p class="case-intro">I also revised competency standards used for employee progression. The underlying 0–3 mastery scale stayed intact; the content became more specific to the work, the operating environment, and the capabilities employees were actually expected to demonstrate.</p>

    <div class="competency-before-after">
      <article class="competency-sheet competency-before">
        <div class="competency-label">Before / inherited standard</div>
        <h3>Check & Straightening</h3>
        <div class="competency-row"><span>Documentation</span><strong>Correctly interpret work instructions, traveler & tech cards</strong><i>0 1 2 3</i></div>
        <div class="competency-row"><span>Tracking</span><strong>Production tracking “EPMS”</strong><i>0 1 2 3</i></div>
        <div class="competency-row"><span>Quality</span><strong>Visually inspect & verify documentation</strong><i>0 1 2 3</i></div>
        <div class="competency-row"><span>Priority</span><strong>Evaluating work priority</strong><i>0 1 2 3</i></div>
      </article>

      <div class="competency-arrow">
        <span>More operational specificity</span>
        <b>→</b>
        <span>Clearer progression requirements</span>
      </div>

      <article class="competency-sheet competency-after">
        <div class="competency-label">After / role-specific standard</div>
        <h3>Vis-Dim & Straightening</h3>
        <div class="competency-row gate"><span>Systems</span><strong>Accurate and consistent use of EPMS for production tracking</strong><i>0 1 2 3</i></div>
        <div class="competency-row gate"><span>Flow</span><strong>Comprehends router/traveler interaction between operations</strong><i>0 1 2 3</i></div>
        <div class="competency-row gate"><span>Certification</span><strong>Pressure Test trained and certified</strong><i>0 1 2 3</i></div>
        <div class="competency-row"><span>Leadership</span><strong>Interdepartmental and shift handoff communication</strong><i>0 1 2 3</i></div>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="competency-notes">
      <div><strong>0–3 mastery scale retained</strong><span>0 = no skill; 3 = high skill with no assistance required.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Progression gates retained and clarified</strong><span>Critical highlighted competencies require a rating of 3 before advancement, regardless of total score.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Framework adapted across roles</strong><span>Inspection & straightening · Caustic processing · Cutoff operations</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section case-alt">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">05</div>
    <div class="eyebrow">Built for a Live Operation</div>
    <h2>Structure without rigidity.</h2>
    <div class="decision-grid training-decisions">
      <article><span>01</span><h3>Plan around real work.</h3><p>Training shifted when attendance, trainer availability, or production demand changed instead of simply stopping.</p></article>
      <article><span>02</span><h3>Use feedback as evidence.</h3><p>Employee check-ins and experienced-operator feedback helped identify where additional practice was needed.</p></article>
      <article><span>03</span><h3>Test before the test.</h3><p>Mock assessments surfaced gaps early enough to target the final training period before certification.</p></article>
      <article><span>04</span><h3>Make it transferable.</h3><p>Documented plans and standards allowed another supervisor or lead to understand the intended development path without relying on my memory.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-outcomes training-outcomes">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no light">06</div>
    <div class="eyebrow light">Early Outcomes</div>
    <h2>Eight employees. All certified ahead of historical timelines.</h2>
    <div class="outcome-grid">
      <div><strong>6–8 weeks</strong><span>Fastest certifications</span></div>
      <div><strong>12–14 weeks</strong><span>Longest certifications in the cohort</span></div>
      <div><strong>~6–8+ months</strong><span>Historically reported certification timelines</span></div>
      <div><strong>Reusable</strong><span>Designed for another supervisor to operate</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>Historical timelines are based on reported departmental norms rather than a formal longitudinal dataset, so I present the comparison conservatively rather than calculating a percentage reduction.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section training-related">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">07</div>
    <div class="eyebrow">Related System</div>
    <h2>Developing capability and seeing capability are different problems.</h2>
    <p class="case-intro">This system structures individual development through certification. A separate building-wide system makes existing skills, coverage gaps, and cross-training opportunities visible across the workforce.</p>
    <a class="related-system" href={`${base}/built-systems/cross-training-matrix/`}>
      <span>Explore the separate system</span>
      <strong>Cross-Training & Workforce Capability Dashboard</strong>
      <b>→</b>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-next">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="eyebrow">Next System</span>
    <a href={`${base}/built-systems/cross-training-matrix/`}>
      <strong>Cross-Training & Workforce Capability Dashboard</strong><span>→</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>
</BaseLayout>
